From 7da28b2e4357a6946f9297e7aca468fded391990 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hoangvu12 <hggaming91@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 19:35:56 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] A stream on the direct link widens its window, so a file
 crosses a long link at full speed

Each preview mux stream had a fixed 64 KiB receive window: one window a
round trip, about 0.7 MB/s across the 89 ms between the person's two
computers, where plain TCP carried 11 MB/s. Behind it the DataChannel's
256 KiB send buffer and the 512 KiB SCTP receive buffer capped any window
at about 2.9 MB/s.

- A sender that runs out of window asks for more (a MORE frame, behind
  what it sent, at most once per 10 ms). The receiver answers once all
  that came before the ask has gone to its reader, doubling the stream's
  window up to 1 MiB (mux::MAX_WINDOW: a 90 ms round trip at about
  11 MB/s). A reader that stopped never gets that far and keeps the
  window it had.
- Only between two ends that both said they take more: each sends a GROW
  frame first, on stream 0, saying the most credit it takes. An end from
  before ignores frames for stream 0, so it never hears of it, never gets
  credit past 64 KiB (which it would end the connection over) and never
  gets a MORE frame (an unknown kind, same). Streams with it run as
  before.
- The DataChannel's send buffer is MAX_WINDOW and the SCTP receive buffer
  twice that, so a grown window isn't capped below it.

Tests (mux, over a stand-in link that delays each frame):
a_window_grows_to_fill_a_long_link: 2 MiB across a 90 ms round trip took
2.96 s at 64 KiB (0.71 MB/s) and 0.56 s (3.77 MB/s) grown, five runs
alike;
an_end_from_before_keeps_its_window_and_the_link: new to old, old to new
and old to old, 1 MiB each, the connection held;
a_reader_that_stopped_holds_back_its_window. Not measured between two
real computers yet.
